label chapter_3:
    
    scene sky with corner_fade

    pause 2.0

    scene black with Dissolve(1.0)

    pause    

    narrator "I felt a bit lighter during the next climb up to the dandelion field."
    narrator "The sky was bright blue and clear without a cloud in the sky, with just enough of a breeze to rustle the heavy heads of the dandelions."
    
    pause 0.5

    narrator "Tomorrow, Julie and Anne would meet."
    narrator "I wondered what kind of strange things might come from that."
    narrator "Maybe Julie would have the same starstuck response to Anne's appearance as she had to my wedding stories."
    narrator "... Oh. I hope I didn't embarrass her, with that one."

    narrator "I tried to push that thought out of my head, rubbing the sweat off the back of my neck as I sat down on one of the long, flat stones."

    scene sky with down_fade

    pause 1.0

    
    narrator "I laid on my back, staring into the expanse of the blue sky."
    narrator "It was even bluer than the dress Julie had worn, yesterday, now that I thought about it."
    narrator "That made me want to laugh, a little."

    pause 2.0

    scene black
    with down_fade

    narrator "I closed my eyes, and waited."
    
    pause 2.0

    scene black with Dissolve(2.0)

    pause 3.0

    narrator "... Huh?"

    narrator "Julie and I had never set a proper meeting time, but she always seemed to show up just as I was climbing the path to this field."
    narrator "Was I late? Or early?"

    scene dandelion_field with Dissolve(0.4)

    mark "\"... It's the same time as before.\""

    narrator "I laid in the grass, waiting for even a single cloud to appear in the sky, or hoping to hear the rustle of someone wading through the grass..."

    pause 1.0

    scene black with Dissolve(0.5)

    pause

    narrator "... But no one appeared."

    pause 0.5

    mark "\"I wonder what happened to her...\""

    narrator "Now that I thought about it, I wasn't even sure where Julie lived."
    narrator "She'd claimed to be from the Cove City of the future.{w} Did that mean she was really one of the people in that settlement, far below where I was standing?"

    mark "\"... ... ...\""

    narrator "That didn't seem right, somehow."

    narrator "I didn't have a phone number, and even if I had, the ranger station and its phone were at least an hour away."

    pause 1.0

    scene sky with down_fade

    pause 

    narrator "I sat back down, and stared up at the sky."

    narrator "Just a day ago, I was staring at all kinds of white shapes in the sky, trying to make them into proper images."
    narrator "... But now, there wasn't a single cloud in the sky."

    narrator "The clouds, and Julie, had both gone."

    scene black with Dissolve(3.0)

    pause

    stop music fadeout 2.0

    narrator "I spent the rest of my day in the dandelion field, lazing about and pacing back and forth."
    narrator "I thought that, maybe... if I waited, Julie Danvers would eventually reappear."
    narrator "But as the sky began to bleed an orange-red, and the sound of the crickets returned... I realized Julie wouldn't be back, today."

    play music crickets fadein 1.0

    pause 3.0
    
    play sound footsteps_in_grass
    scene moon_night_sky
    with down_fade

    narrator "Instead of returning to the cabin, I walked back to the entrance of the camp grounds, then set off for the ranger's station."

    narrator "... But that wasn't right, either."

    pause 1.0

    scene black with Dissolve(1.4)

    narrator "The young man at the counter, looking a bit more annoyed than usual, let out a big yawn and grumbled, 'something funny going on with the telephone.'"
    narrator "He wasn't wrong.{w} When I picked it up, there wasn't even a dial tone."
    narrator "The kid promised someone was coming by to take a look at it tomorrow, but tonight there was nothing he could do."
    narrator "He gave me a weary look, and said 'Yeah... real sorry about that.'"
    narrator "So that night, I didn't speak to my wife."

    pause 1.0

    play sound footsteps_in_grass
    scene moon_night_sky
    with Dissolve(1.0)

    narrator "I walked along the beaten path to the cabin, all by myself."
    narrator "The sound of the crickets, which were normally loud, seemed less present than usual."
    narrator "Even the moon, which would normally be hanging low in the sky, seemed to be hidden away behind dark gray clouds."

    pause 2.0

    scene outro_3
    with Dissolve(1.0)

    pause 3.0

    scene black with Dissolve(4.0)
    

    pause 5.0

    play music seasons fadein 2.0

    narrator "The next day, Anne arrived."
    narrator "She looked pretty cranky when she ambled out of Bern's old van, but lit up as soon as she saw me."
    
    scene black with custom_hpunch

    anne "\"Maaaaaark!\""    

    narrator "Anne wrapped me up in her arms and squeezed me tight, so tight that I actually heard something creak in my joints."
    narrator "Urgh...!"

    pause 1.0

    narrator "Bern, who'd been watching us embrace with wide eyes, giggled at my strained expression."

    narrator "We entered the cabin, and Anne made tea for all three of us."

    narrator "The process of taking care of Bern's mother hadn't been an easy one.{w} Bern kept making a painful face, and apologizing over and over for taking Anne away from me for the last few days."

    narrator "Of course, it was silly for her to apologize."
    
    narrator "If the shoe was on the other foot, I was sure she would've helped Anne out, too."

    pause 2.0

    narrator "We drank tea, sat by the fire, and chatted for a time until Bern let out a yawn and said she had to begin driving back."

    narrator "She looked at Anne, who was curled around my arm, and gave me a warm smile."

    mark "\"... G-glad to see you again, Bern...\""

    narrator "I had a hard time looking her in the eye with my wife glued to me so closely, but she didn't seem to mind at all."

    narrator "She grinned at the two of us, helped clean up the remains of the tea set, and a few minutes later, she waved us off and she was gone."

    pause 2.0

    anne "\"I see you did a bit of organizing while I was away.\""
    
    mark "\"Huh?\""
    
    anne "\"Did you really have to rearrange the entire room, though...?{w} I thought it looked so cozy being a little messy...\""

    narrator "Anne let out a sigh, and gave my arm a gentle squeeze."

    anne "\"Well, it's fine...{w} I'm glad you kept busy, until now.\""

    narrator "Anne sounded satisfied with that."

    narrator "She closed her eyes... and smiled."

    pause 1.0

    narrator "I placed a hand on her head, and that smile widened."

    narrator "That's right.{w} I'd... missed this woman, so much."

    pause 3.0

    anne "\"Hmmm... so she wasn't there, today?\""

    narrator "I told Anne about how Julie had disappeared the next day, and mentioned the broken phone at the ranger's station while I was at it."

    narrator "She listened carefully, nodding her head, and then paused."

    pause 1.0

    anne "\"Hmm...{w} Well, it's a bit late to go out again, but why don't we go back tomorrow and see if we can find her?\""

    mark "\"Sure, I'd like that.\""

    narrator "Anne grinned."

    anne "\"Hehe. I've been wanting to meet this strange girl, since you met her.\""

    
    narrator "The next day, we did just that."

    narrator "We made out way up the same path, with Anne telling me to mind my footing, until we finally reached the familiar field."

    narrator "Like before... I didn't see any trace of Julie Danvers."

    anne "\"Ooooohhhh! It's so pretty...\""

    narrator "It definitely was."

    narrator "Even though Julie wasn't here, Anne looked captivated by the view."

    narrator "The sight made Anne's eyes sparkle, and together, we gazed down at Cove City in the distance."

    pause 2.0

    anne "\"They really do look like ants down there, huh...\""

    narrator "Anne wrinkled her brow, as if she was squinting to try to see better."

    mark "\"Wonder if that's where she was from, in the end.\""

    anne "\"Hmmm...\""

    narrator "Anne took a seat on an old log, and thought about that."

    anne "\"Maybe... it's quite a ways away, isn't it?{w} Could she really walk all that way every day...?\""

    narrator "I hadn't thought about that."

    narrator "Had Julie managed to find another way here?{w} And in that case... was she not here because that wasn't possible anymore?"

    narrator "It was only dawning on me now how little I knew about Julie Danvers."

    narrator "There was... a tiny part of me that wondered if I'd imagined the whole thing."

    narrator "But the smile on Anne's face made me think that probably hadn't even entered her mind."

    anne "\"Well... it's too bad.{w} I guess today you're just stuck with me.\""

    mark "\"I'm not mad about that at all.\""

    anne "\"Hehe... that's a good answer.\""

    narrator "Anne wrapped an arm around my waist, and pressed her body to mine."

    narrator "I wondered what happened to the dandelion girl, and a part of me was unsure of what the entire thing had amounted to, in the first place."

    narrator "But with Anne beside me, closing her eyes and enjoying the sunshine streaming down on us, I decided to stop trying to think about it any further."

    ## Time passes ##

    pause 3.0

    narrator "The next three days I spent with my wife."

    narrator "I had fun, making up the time we'd lost the last few days."

    narrator "We played cards, roasted marshmallows, and she burst into a laughing fit when I fell asleep with a book in my lap."

    narrator "Time flowed much more quickly than it ever had before."

    narrator "I started to forget my disappointment over Julie Danvers' disappearance, and I enjoyed that remaining time with just me and my wife."


    pause 0.5


    narrator "Toward the end... We stopped visiting that field, and we spent the last day laughing and reminiscing about the past before we had to start packing up and thinking about going back."

    narrator "I didn't encounter Julie Danvers again, during my vacation."

    pause 2.0

    anne "\"Hey, Mark.\""

    narrator "On the drive home, I felt my wife's hand on my shoulder."

    mark "\"Hmm? What's going on?\""

    anne "\"Are you feeling OK?\""

    mark "\"Yeah, why do you ask?\""

    narrator "For a moment, Anne's eyes flickered between me and the rear-view mirror."

    anne "\"Hmm... you keep looking back at the camp ground.\""

    narrator "It was true, I'd glanced back in the mirror several times and watched it getting smaller and smaller behind us."

    narrator "I'd be lying if I said I didn't feel a little melancholy about that."

    pause 2.0

    mark "\"Thinking of how much I'll miss roasting marshmallows with you until next year.\""

    anne "\"Hehe. Maybe we can manage something in the meantime.\""
    
    narrator "My wife smiled, curled around my arm and closed her eyes as the road expanded and the countryside began to disappear behind us."


    pause 3.0

    
    narrator "That wasn't the last time I visited the camp grounds before our next vacation."

    narrator "For some reason, something bothered me."

    narrator "The unexpected disappearance of Julie Danvers felt... wrong."

    narrator "What had happened to that bubbly girl, who'd looked so thrilled to see me each time we crossed paths?"

    narrator "Once in a blue moon, I'd drive back to the camp ground and make the slow walk to the dandelion field where Julie and I had first met."

    narrator "Once or twice I went with Anne, and we sat in the grass eating lunch, but I usually went alone."

    narrator "Each time, I felt my heart swell with hope...{w} only for it to be quashed again."

    pause 3.0

    narrator "But then."

    narrator "One day... months later, I saw a familiar face, standing perfectly still among the growing, late Summer weeds."

    narrator "I had to do a double take, because that face looked pale and strained."

    pause 1.0

    mark "\"Julie...?\""

    narrator "I held my brother, wondering if this was just my eyes playing tricks on me."

    pause 1.0

    narrator "Julie didn't turn around.{w} Instead... her entire body shifted, just ever so slightly, and she glanced at me."

    narrator "Even from this angle, I could see how exhausted she looked."

    narrator "Just as quickly as she'd looked, she turned back again."

    pause 1.5

    julie "\"Finally... I was thinking we'd never meet again.\""

    mark "\"Julie...{w} What happened to you...?\""

    pause 3.0

    narrator "Julie craned her head upward, and stared at the sky.{w} It was beginning to bleed orange-red, and the clouds had mostly gone."

    julie "\"I was... so stupid.\""

    pause 1.0

    julie "\"... Papa was always in poor health.{w} He never slept enough, and he was always forgetting to eat.{w} So... of course, I didn't notice when he began to get worse.\""

    julie "\"He was so wrapped up in his research, and I was so selfishly jumping back and forth through time, that I never even thought about it.\""

    mark "\"Julie...\""

    julie "\"... It's been so hard, finding this exact moment.{w} Papa's time machine has been wearing down, and I can't fix it all on my own...{w} Eventually I remembered the coordinates, and found this field and this time again... but you stopped coming.\""

    mark "\"... ... ...\""

    julie "Us meeting is just luck. There was... the possibility that this jump would be meaningless, and I'd miss you again. {w} ... No, maybe it's fate. Time is meant to play out a certain way, after all.\""

    narrator "Julie lowered her head.{w} She squeezed her fists at her side."

    julie "\"... I hate that. What a stupid joke.{w} ... It's meant to be like this? I'm meant to screw up, and not even notice Papa was sick?{w} To forget where and when this place is, and miss you... ?\""

    narrator "I could feel the bitterness dripping from Julie's voice."

    julie "\"I hate it...\""

    narrator "In the time between then and now, Julie had lost something precious to her."

    narrator "That loss, for whatever reason, had put so much distance between her and I."

    pause 2.0

    julie "\"... Where am I supposed to go, now?{w} There's nothing back home for me, now, except for an empty aparment full of old dusty books.\""

    narrator "Julie closed her eyes, and I felt all of her will ooze out of her body."

    julie "\"Mr. Mark...{w} What am I supposed to do, now?\""


    pause 2.0

    
    narrator "I acted without thinking about it."

    narrator "I wrapped my arms around Julie Danvers, and held her tightly."

    julie "\"Uu... un... ghhh... uuuwaaaahhhhhhhhhh...\""

    narrator "A dam finally burst open, and Julie cried."

    narrator "She'd said that there was nothing left for her but an empty apartment."

    narrator "Did that mean that after her father was gone, she'd had nothing to do but curl up in a ball by herself and cry?"

    narrator "I couldn't begin to even imagine how painful that must be.{w} It didn't feel right for her to have to suffer like that alone."

    
    

    

    

    


    

    pause

            
